  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1006",
      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Denticetopsis praecox (Ferraris &amp; Brown, 1991)</li><li>Synonym: Pseudocetopsis praecox</li><li>Size: 53 mm (2.1\") SL</li><li>IUCN Red List: Not Evaluated</li><li>pH: 4.3 - 5.0</li></ul><p>Among the smallest species in the family, maturing between 30-40 mm SL.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1006",
      "html": "<p>South America: upper Negro River basin. Type locality: Neblina base camp, Río Mawarinuma, Río Baria drainage, Territory Fed. Amazonas, southern Venezuela, 0°55'N, 66°10'W, elevation 120 meters.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1006",
      "html": "<p>Known from cobble-pebble bottom river habitats.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1006",
      "html": "<p>Males are smaller and have filament extensions to the dorsal and often pectoral fins.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1006",
      "html": "<p>Unreported.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1006",
      "html": "<p>Readily distinguished from other members of the genus by its colour pattern consisting of a dark brown back, speckled to dark sides, light belly, pale fins and a large dark spot on the basal half of the dorsal fin.</p>"
    },
